1
00:00:00,05 --> 00:00:02,08
- [Instructor] Before we get started on installing Mongo,

2
00:00:02,08 --> 00:00:05,03
for those who aren't familiar with Mongo,

3
00:00:05,03 --> 00:00:08,06
here's a quick refresher on what it is.

4
00:00:08,06 --> 00:00:10,06
MongoDB is a database system

5
00:00:10,06 --> 00:00:13,04
that organizes data into collections,

6
00:00:13,04 --> 00:00:15,03
which consist of documents

7
00:00:15,03 --> 00:00:18,09
or objects resembling JSON objects.

8
00:00:18,09 --> 00:00:21,02
These documents contain data structured

9
00:00:21,02 --> 00:00:24,00
in key value pairs or arrays,

10
00:00:24,00 --> 00:00:26,02
allowing for the efficient organization

11
00:00:26,02 --> 00:00:28,03
and retrieval of data.

12
00:00:28,03 --> 00:00:31,03
An example provided is a player's collection

13
00:00:31,03 --> 00:00:33,00
where each player is represented

14
00:00:33,00 --> 00:00:35,07
as a document within this collection.

15
00:00:35,07 --> 00:00:38,01
This structure supports a flexible

16
00:00:38,01 --> 00:00:41,00
and intuitive approach to date management,

17
00:00:41,00 --> 00:00:44,01
catering to a wide range of applications.

18
00:00:44,01 --> 00:00:47,03
So now let's get started on installing Mongo for a server.

19
00:00:47,03 --> 00:00:49,00
In order to be able to use Mongo,

20
00:00:49,00 --> 00:00:51,00
you need to have it installed locally

21
00:00:51,00 --> 00:00:53,08
or use the Atlas cloud-based version.

22
00:00:53,08 --> 00:00:56,09
Feel free to use the cloud-based version if you want.

23
00:00:56,09 --> 00:00:59,09
But for this project, I'll use the local one.

24
00:00:59,09 --> 00:01:02,01
All you have to do is first of all,

25
00:01:02,01 --> 00:01:06,03
look for the Install MongoDB Community page.

26
00:01:06,03 --> 00:01:08,02
So you can search for this on Google,

27
00:01:08,02 --> 00:01:09,08
and once you've found that page,

28
00:01:09,08 --> 00:01:11,00
all you have to do is go

29
00:01:11,00 --> 00:01:13,06
for the operating system that you have

30
00:01:13,06 --> 00:01:16,00
and install it on your system.

31
00:01:16,00 --> 00:01:18,02
So for example, I have macOS,

32
00:01:18,02 --> 00:01:21,09
so I would click here and then follow the instructions.

33
00:01:21,09 --> 00:01:23,08
I'm not going to go through all the instructions

34
00:01:23,08 --> 00:01:26,02
'cause they're well explained here on the website.

35
00:01:26,02 --> 00:01:29,03
So feel free to go ahead and install it on your system.

36
00:01:29,03 --> 00:01:31,05
So you have Windows here.

37
00:01:31,05 --> 00:01:32,04
You have Linux,

38
00:01:32,04 --> 00:01:35,06
and if you want to have a container running Mongo,

39
00:01:35,06 --> 00:01:38,03
you can install it on Docker right here.

40
00:01:38,03 --> 00:01:39,06
Once you have it installed,

41
00:01:39,06 --> 00:01:41,09
and just to make sure that it's working properly

42
00:01:41,09 --> 00:01:42,07
in the background

43
00:01:42,07 --> 00:01:46,00
'cause otherwise, once we start putting the server together,

44
00:01:46,00 --> 00:01:48,07
you're going to have errors running in your console,

45
00:01:48,07 --> 00:01:53,09
what you have to do is go to this webpage, robomongo.org

46
00:01:53,09 --> 00:01:56,06
and installs Studio 3T.

47
00:01:56,06 --> 00:01:57,09
And once you have it installed,

48
00:01:57,09 --> 00:02:00,06
what you can do is literally open it

49
00:02:00,06 --> 00:02:02,06
and open a connection

50
00:02:02,06 --> 00:02:06,06
to localhost port 27017.

51
00:02:06,06 --> 00:02:09,00
This is where Mongo usually runs.

52
00:02:09,00 --> 00:02:11,09
So I can actually click on a connection here,

53
00:02:11,09 --> 00:02:15,04
or you can create a brand new connection by clicking here.

54
00:02:15,04 --> 00:02:18,00
So what you could do is create a new connection

55
00:02:18,00 --> 00:02:19,05
and then add to it.

56
00:02:19,05 --> 00:02:20,06
So let's say, for example,

57
00:02:20,06 --> 00:02:23,03
you didn't have MongoDB already installed.

58
00:02:23,03 --> 00:02:24,08
You can install it manually,

59
00:02:24,08 --> 00:02:26,09
or you can paste the connections here.

60
00:02:26,09 --> 00:02:31,01
But usually, this is the DB server where it's running,

61
00:02:31,01 --> 00:02:33,09
and what you can do, you connect to it,

62
00:02:33,09 --> 00:02:37,03
and if it's connected, and you see the collections here,

63
00:02:37,03 --> 00:02:39,00
you're good to go.

